[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Kubernetes Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "What Kubernetes Is and Why Container Orchestration Matters",
        "content": "Kubernetes is an open-source container orchestration platform that automates the deployment, scheduling, scaling, networking, and lifecycle management of containerized applications. Running containers directly with Docker can work for a small application, but production systems often contain many services, replicas, dependencies, and machines. Kubernetes provides a declarative control system where engineers describe the desired state of an application, such as the number of replicas, container image, resource requirements, and networking configuration. Kubernetes continuously works to make the actual cluster state match that declared state. If a container crashes, a controller can create a replacement. If a worker node becomes unavailable, workloads may be rescheduled onto healthy nodes when capacity and scheduling constraints allow. Kubernetes does not replace application development or container image creation. Instead, it provides a consistent platform for operating containerized workloads across infrastructure environments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Understanding Clusters, Nodes, Pods, and Workloads",
        "content": "A Kubernetes cluster is a collection of control-plane services and worker nodes that cooperate to run applications. A node is a machine, virtual machine, or cloud-provided compute instance that contributes resources to the cluster. A Pod is the smallest deployable Kubernetes object and represents one or more closely related containers that share networking and may share storage. Containers inside the same Pod communicate through localhost and are scheduled together. Most application containers are managed through higher-level workload controllers rather than created as standalone Pods. A Deployment manages stateless application replicas and supports controlled updates. StatefulSets provide stable identities and storage relationships for stateful workloads. DaemonSets run a Pod on selected nodes, commonly for monitoring or networking agents. Jobs and CronJobs handle finite and scheduled tasks. Understanding these resource relationships helps engineers select the correct Kubernetes object instead of using Pods directly for every application requirement."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Declarative Configuration and Desired-State Reconciliation",
        "content": "Kubernetes uses declarative configuration to describe the state an application or infrastructure resource should maintain. A manifest commonly specifies an API version, resource kind, metadata, and specification. For example, a Deployment manifest can request three replicas of a particular container image and define resource requests, limits, labels, and health probes. When the manifest is submitted to the API Server, Kubernetes stores the desired configuration and controllers continuously compare that desired state with the observed cluster state. If one replica disappears, the Deployment and ReplicaSet controllers work to restore the requested replica count. This behavior is called reconciliation. It is different from a one-time command that starts a container and then stops managing it. Declarative workflows make changes reviewable, repeatable, and suitable for Git-based delivery. Teams should keep manifests in source control, validate them before deployment, and avoid uncontrolled manual changes that create drift between Git and the running cluster."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Kubernetes API Objects, Manifests, and Resource Relationships",
        "content": "Kubernetes manages cluster resources through API objects. Each object has an API group and version where applicable, a kind, metadata, and a specification describing its desired configuration. Common objects include Pods, Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, Secrets, Namespaces, PersistentVolumeClaims, and NetworkPolicies. Metadata supports identification and organization through names, labels, and annotations. Labels are especially important because selectors use them to associate related resources. A Deployment manages ReplicaSets, and a ReplicaSet manages Pods matching its selector. A Service selects matching Pods and provides a stable network endpoint even when individual Pod IP addresses change. ConfigMaps and Secrets provide configuration data to workloads through supported consumption methods. Engineers should understand these relationships before modifying manifests, because an incorrect label selector can leave a Service without endpoints or cause a controller to manage the wrong Pods. Use kubectl explain and API documentation to inspect resource fields and supported versions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Container Images, Registries, and Kubernetes Image Delivery",
        "content": "Kubernetes runs containers from images stored in container registries. A Pod specification identifies the image repository and tag or digest, while the node's container runtime retrieves the required image when it is not already available locally. Production environments commonly use private registries such as Amazon ECR, Docker Hub private repositories, or enterprise registries. The cluster must have a supported authentication mechanism and network access to retrieve the image. An incorrect image name, missing tag, inaccessible registry, or insufficient permission can produce an ImagePullBackOff condition. Mutable tags can make deployments difficult to reproduce because the same tag may refer to different content over time. Digest-based image references identify a specific image artifact and improve release traceability. Teams should build and scan images in CI/CD, publish approved artifacts, and deploy known versions. Image pull permissions should be granted to the correct node or workload retrieval identity according to the Kubernetes platform and cloud integration being used."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Scaling Applications and Maintaining Workload Availability",
        "content": "Kubernetes supports application scaling by changing the number of workload replicas or by using autoscaling mechanisms. A Deployment can increase its replica count to run more copies of a stateless application, provided the cluster has enough scheduling capacity. The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler can adjust replica counts using configured metrics such as CPU utilization or supported custom metrics. Cluster-level autoscaling mechanisms can add or remove worker capacity when scheduling demand changes, depending on the infrastructure provider and configuration. Replicas improve availability only when workloads can be placed across suitable failure domains and the application itself supports multiple instances. Pod disruption budgets can help protect availability during voluntary disruptions, while readiness probes determine whether a Pod should receive normal Service traffic. Scaling also depends on resource requests, limits, application startup time, storage behavior, and downstream service capacity. Production teams should test scaling behavior under realistic traffic and avoid assuming that increasing replicas automatically resolves every performance bottleneck."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Kubernetes Networking and Service Discovery Fundamentals",
        "content": "Kubernetes provides a networking model in which Pods receive IP addresses and can communicate according to the cluster network implementation and configured network policies. Pod IP addresses are generally not stable across replacement, so applications should not depend on a specific Pod address. A Service provides a stable virtual endpoint and selects backend Pods using labels. ClusterIP is commonly used for internal cluster communication, while NodePort and LoadBalancer expose services through additional networking mechanisms. DNS-based service discovery allows workloads to resolve service names instead of maintaining changing IP addresses. Ingress resources and Gateway API implementations can provide HTTP or application routing through compatible controllers and infrastructure. Network policies can restrict Pod communication when the cluster networking implementation supports them. Engineers should understand the difference between Pod networking, Service routing, external load balancing, and DNS resolution when diagnosing connectivity. A service with no ready endpoints may indicate a selector mismatch, failing readiness probe, or unavailable Pods rather than a network outage."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Storage, Configuration, and Application Health Management",
        "content": "Kubernetes separates application configuration, sensitive values, and persistent data from the container image. ConfigMaps hold non-sensitive configuration, while Secrets are intended for sensitive values and require appropriate access controls and encryption practices. Applications can consume configuration through environment variables or mounted volumes, depending on the resource definition and update behavior. PersistentVolumeClaims request storage from the cluster, while PersistentVolumes represent storage resources made available through the configured storage system. Storage behavior depends on the provisioner, StorageClass, access mode, and underlying storage capabilities. Applications should also define startup, readiness, and liveness probes according to their actual behavior. A startup probe can allow a slow application to initialize before other health checks become active. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic, while liveness can trigger container restarts when the configured failure condition is met. Poorly designed probes can cause unnecessary restarts or send traffic to an application that is not ready."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Operating Kubernetes with kubectl and Production Best Practices",
        "content": "kubectl is the standard command-line client for communicating with the Kubernetes API Server. Engineers use it to inspect cluster resources, apply manifests, retrieve logs, execute commands in containers, and diagnose workload conditions. Common commands include kubectl get, describe, apply, logs, exec, rollout, and top when metrics support is available. Always confirm the active context and namespace before running commands that modify production resources. Use namespaces and RBAC to separate teams and workloads, and grant only the permissions needed for each operational role. Store manifests in source control and use automated validation before applying changes. Monitor node health, Pod restarts, resource consumption, deployment progress, and application-level service indicators. Avoid treating kubectl apply as a complete deployment strategy without release validation, rollback planning, and change tracking. Production readiness also requires tested backups for relevant stateful data, secure image sources, network controls, resource planning, and documented incident procedures. These practices make Kubernetes operations more predictable and safer at scale."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Kubernetes Architecture",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "Kubernetes Control Plane and Worker Node Architecture",
        "content": "Kubernetes architecture separates cluster management responsibilities from the execution of application workloads. The control plane maintains the cluster's desired state, exposes the Kubernetes API, makes scheduling decisions, and coordinates resource reconciliation. Worker nodes provide the compute environment where Pods run. In a self-managed cluster, engineers may operate control-plane components and worker machines themselves. Managed Kubernetes services operate some or most control-plane infrastructure on behalf of the customer, while customers remain responsible for workload configuration and many cluster-level decisions. A typical architecture includes the API Server, etcd, scheduler, controller manager, kubelet, container runtime, and networking components. These components cooperate through the Kubernetes API and node-level communication. Understanding the separation of responsibilities is essential when diagnosing failures. For example, a healthy API Server does not guarantee that worker nodes can retrieve images, and healthy worker nodes do not guarantee that the scheduler can place new Pods. Production architecture must account for control-plane availability, worker capacity, network connectivity, identity, storage, and failure domains."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Kubernetes API Server and Request Processing Lifecycle",
        "content": "The Kubernetes API Server is the central entry point for cluster management operations. kubectl, controllers, automation systems, and other clients send API requests to it using authenticated and authorized connections. The API Server validates requests, performs admission processing, and coordinates persistence of accepted resource state in etcd. Authentication establishes the identity of the caller, while authorization determines whether that identity can perform the requested action on a resource. Admission controllers can validate or modify requests according to configured policies before persistence. This request path is important for security and troubleshooting because a request can fail at authentication, authorization, admission, validation, or storage. For example, an RBAC denial differs from an invalid manifest field or a failed admission policy. Engineers should inspect the actual API response and audit evidence rather than assuming every kubectl error indicates a control-plane outage. The API Server also provides discovery and resource endpoints used by clients and controllers to understand the available Kubernetes API surface."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "etcd as the Kubernetes Cluster State Database",
        "content": "etcd is a distributed key-value data store used by Kubernetes to persist cluster state. Important information about Kubernetes API objects, including desired workload configuration and metadata, is stored through the API Server's persistence layer. The control plane relies on etcd availability and consistency to manage cluster state correctly. In self-managed environments, etcd deployment, member health, quorum, backup, encryption, and recovery are critical operational responsibilities. A multi-member etcd cluster can tolerate certain member failures while maintaining quorum, but losing quorum can prevent normal write operations. Managed Kubernetes offerings typically operate the control-plane data store as part of the service, so customers do not directly administer the provider's etcd infrastructure. However, they remain responsible for backing up application data and any cluster configuration that must be recoverable through their own processes. Never treat etcd as a substitute for application database backups. Understand the difference between Kubernetes control-plane state recovery and restoring persistent data used by applications."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Kubernetes Scheduler and Pod Placement Decisions",
        "content": "The Kubernetes scheduler assigns newly created Pods to suitable worker nodes. It evaluates resource requests, node capacity, scheduling constraints, affinity and anti-affinity rules, taints and tolerations, topology requirements, and other scheduling conditions. A Pod that cannot be placed remains pending while the scheduler waits for a suitable node or a change in cluster conditions. Resource requests are particularly important because they represent the capacity Kubernetes uses when evaluating placement. A node may appear to have free memory at the operating-system level but still lack allocatable capacity for the requested workload. Taints can repel Pods unless they have matching tolerations, while node selectors and affinity rules can restrict eligible nodes. Topology spread constraints can help distribute replicas across zones or nodes. Engineers should inspect Pod events with kubectl describe pod when scheduling fails. Increasing the replica count or changing node size without understanding the scheduling event may not solve the actual constraint. Effective capacity planning combines realistic resource requests with workload placement requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "Controller Manager and the Reconciliation Architecture",
        "content": "Kubernetes controllers continuously observe cluster resources and work to move actual state toward desired state. The controller manager runs several control loops responsible for different resource types and cluster functions. For example, a Deployment controller coordinates ReplicaSets, while a ReplicaSet controller ensures that the expected number of matching Pods exists. Other controllers manage endpoints, namespaces, service accounts, and node-related behavior depending on the cluster configuration. Controllers generally react to observed API state rather than executing a fixed sequence of commands only once. If a Pod disappears, the relevant controller notices the replica count difference and creates a replacement through the API. Reconciliation can take time, and it does not guarantee that every requested state is achievable. Missing resources, insufficient capacity, invalid specifications, or external dependency failures can prevent convergence. Engineers should inspect the controlling resource and its events rather than manually recreating managed Pods. Understanding controller ownership prevents conflicting manual actions and makes declarative operations more reliable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "Kubelet and Node-Level Pod Lifecycle Management",
        "content": "The kubelet is the primary node agent responsible for ensuring that Pods assigned to its node are running according to their specifications. It communicates with the API Server, observes Pod assignments, and coordinates container lifecycle operations through the configured container runtime. The kubelet also manages volume mounting, probe execution, and reporting node and Pod status. If a node loses communication with the control plane, the API Server may eventually mark the node unhealthy while the kubelet and running containers may continue operating locally for some time. Node-level failures can affect image retrieval, container startup, storage mounting, and health reporting. Engineers should inspect node conditions, kubelet logs, runtime health, disk pressure, memory pressure, and network connectivity when Pods fail on a particular worker. In managed services, access to the underlying kubelet may be restricted, so use the supported node diagnostics and provider logging facilities. Understanding kubelet responsibilities helps distinguish application failures from problems in the node's container execution layer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Container Runtime and CRI Integration",
        "content": "Kubernetes uses a container runtime on worker nodes to create and manage containers. The Container Runtime Interface provides the integration contract between kubelet and supported runtimes. Common runtime implementations include containerd and CRI-O. Docker Engine was historically used in many Kubernetes environments, but Kubernetes removed its built-in dockershim integration in version 1.24; this did not mean that Docker-built images stopped working. OCI-compatible images can be used by supported runtimes when the image format and platform requirements are satisfied. The runtime is responsible for operations such as pulling images, creating containers, starting processes, and reporting container status through the CRI integration. Runtime failures can cause Pods to remain in creation states or fail during container startup even when the API Server and scheduler are healthy. Engineers should inspect runtime service health, node logs, image availability, storage capacity, and CRI compatibility. Avoid assuming that kubectl and Docker CLI commands provide identical views of every container on a Kubernetes node."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "Kubernetes Networking Components and Service Routing",
        "content": "Kubernetes networking connects Pods, Services, nodes, and external clients through a combination of cluster networking and service-routing components. A Container Network Interface plugin provides Pod networking according to the cluster's implementation. Service routing may be handled by kube-proxy or an alternative supported data plane. kube-proxy watches Service and EndpointSlice information and configures node networking rules to direct traffic to appropriate backends. Different implementations may use iptables, IPVS, or other mechanisms depending on the environment and configuration. CoreDNS commonly provides service discovery by resolving cluster DNS names. In managed cloud clusters, additional cloud networking components may assign Pod addresses, integrate load balancers, or enforce network policy. A connectivity failure can occur at several layers: DNS resolution, Pod routing, Service selection, node rules, security groups, network ACLs, or external load balancer configuration. Troubleshooting should test each layer separately and verify that Service selectors match ready Pods. Architecture diagrams should show the actual networking implementation rather than assuming every cluster uses identical components."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "Control Plane High Availability and Failure Domains",
        "content": "Production Kubernetes architecture must consider control-plane availability and the impact of failures across infrastructure and network boundaries. A highly available control plane distributes critical management services across suitable failure domains, depending on the Kubernetes distribution or managed service design. In self-managed clusters, engineers must plan API Server availability, etcd quorum, load balancing, certificates, backups, and recovery procedures. In managed services, the cloud provider operates the managed control plane, but customers still need resilient worker capacity and application placement. Worker nodes should be distributed across appropriate zones where the infrastructure and workload design support it. Pod replicas should avoid unnecessary concentration on a single node or failure domain. Pod disruption budgets and topology spread constraints can help reduce the risk of voluntary disruptions or uneven placement. High availability is not achieved merely by running multiple replicas if all replicas depend on one node, one storage system, or one network path. Validate the architecture through failure exercises that test node loss, zone impairment, capacity shortages, and application dependency behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "Tracing an End-to-End Kubernetes Control and Workload Flow",
        "content": "An end-to-end Kubernetes workflow begins when a user or automation client submits a workload manifest to the API Server. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and persisted as desired state. Controllers observe the new object and create dependent resources such as ReplicaSets and Pods. The scheduler selects suitable worker nodes based on resource requests, placement constraints, and node conditions. The API Server records the assignment, and the kubelet on the selected node observes the Pod specification. The kubelet asks the container runtime to retrieve required images and create the containers, while networking and storage components provide the necessary runtime resources. Health probes report application readiness and liveness, and Services route traffic to eligible endpoints. Controllers continue reconciling the workload if replicas disappear or configuration changes. When a deployment fails, engineers can trace the request through API validation, controller activity, scheduling events, node execution, image retrieval, storage mounting, and network routing. This layered mental model supports faster diagnosis because it identifies which component owns each stage of workload creation and operation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "EKS Architecture",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Amazon EKS Architecture Overview",
        "content": "## Amazon EKS Architecture Overview\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on Amazon EKS Architecture Overview. Explain the overall managed Kubernetes architecture. Distinguish the AWS-operated control plane from customer-operated worker capacity, and describe how the cluster API connects these layers. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks describe-cluster --name production-eks --region ap-south-1\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Managed Control Plane and Customer Data Plane",
        "content": "## Managed Control Plane and Customer Data Plane\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on Managed Control Plane and Customer Data Plane. Explain the responsibility boundary between AWS-managed control-plane infrastructure and customer-managed workloads, nodes, networking, and application operations. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks describe-cluster --name production-eks --query cluster.resourcesVpcConfig\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-3",
        "title": "EKS Cluster Provisioning Lifecycle",
        "content": "## EKS Cluster Provisioning Lifecycle\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on EKS Cluster Provisioning Lifecycle. Describe cluster creation, VPC selection, subnet configuration, endpoint provisioning, cluster readiness, node registration, and post-provisioning validation. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks create-cluster --name production-eks --role-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/EKSClusterRole --resources-vpc-config subnetIds=subnet-abc,subnet-def\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-4",
        "title": "EKS Control Plane Availability and Resilience",
        "content": "## EKS Control Plane Availability and Resilience\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on EKS Control Plane Availability and Resilience. Explain managed control-plane availability, multiple availability zones, API endpoint resilience, maintenance responsibilities, and customer-side dependencies. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks describe-cluster --name production-eks --query cluster.status\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-5",
        "title": "EKS Cluster Networking Architecture",
        "content": "## EKS Cluster Networking Architecture\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on EKS Cluster Networking Architecture. Explain VPC integration, cluster subnets, node subnets, Pod addressing, routing, security groups, and communication paths between cluster components. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks describe-cluster --name production-eks --query cluster.resourcesVpcConfig\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-6",
        "title": "Cluster API Endpoint Access Design",
        "content": "## Cluster API Endpoint Access Design\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on Cluster API Endpoint Access Design. Describe public and private Kubernetes API endpoints, endpoint access restrictions, administrator connectivity, private networking, and secure operational access. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks describe-cluster --name production-eks --query cluster.resourcesVpcConfig.endpointPrivateAccess\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-7",
        "title": "IAM Identity and EKS Access Architecture",
        "content": "## IAM Identity and EKS Access Architecture\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on IAM Identity and EKS Access Architecture. Explain IAM authentication, EKS access entries, Kubernetes authorization, administrator roles, least privilege, and the distinction between AWS permissions and Kubernetes permissions. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks list-access-entries --cluster-name production-eks\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful command means the application is healthy. Correlate Kubernetes events, resource conditions, AWS resource state, and application logs. In a production environment, capture the output and timestamp before changing configuration so that the original failure evidence remains available.\n\n### Troubleshooting and failure analysis\nCommon failures include incorrect permissions, incompatible versions, missing network routes, exhausted resources, invalid configuration, unhealthy dependencies, and delayed reconciliation. Identify the failing layer first. Check whether the API request reached the cluster, whether the relevant object was accepted, whether the responsible controller or AWS integration acted, and whether the resulting workload or infrastructure became ready. Avoid deleting resources or changing multiple settings before collecting evidence.\n\n### CI/CD and production engineering\nA reliable delivery pipeline should validate manifests, verify image references, use narrowly scoped deployment identities, apply changes through controlled automation, and wait for rollout or resource readiness. Production teams should maintain version compatibility records, monitor resource capacity, protect secrets, restrict network exposure, and define rollback procedures. Changes to cluster infrastructure should be separated from application releases when their risk and recovery requirements differ.\n\n### Operational checklist\nConfirm ownership boundaries, permissions, dependencies, configuration, resource health, and monitoring. Record the expected state and compare it with the observed state. Verify that the relevant component is available, that its dependent services can communicate, and that failure signals are visible in logs or events. Document the corrective action and validate the application-level outcome before closing an incident."
      },
