        "id": "stage-10-topic-3-section-9",
        "title": "Managed Node Group Architecture",
        "content": "## Managed Node Group Architecture\n\n### Technical explanation\nAmazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer selects and operates workload capacity and configures the AWS resources surrounding the cluster. EKS architecture must be understood across the AWS account, VPC, cluster endpoint, IAM identity, worker compute, networking, storage, and application layers.\n\nThis section focuses on Managed Node Group Architecture. Describe managed node group configuration, launch templates, Auto Scaling groups, node AMIs, instance types, scaling configuration, and rolling updates. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\naws eks describe-nodegroup --cluster-name production-eks --nodegroup-name production-workers\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned object rather than assuming that a successful