        "id": "stage-10-topic-4-section-16",
        "title": "Control Plane Watches, Events, and Reconciliation",
        "content": "## Control Plane Watches, Events, and Reconciliation\n\n### Technical explanation\nThe Kubernetes control plane is responsible for accepting API requests, storing cluster state, making scheduling decisions, and reconciling resources toward their declared configuration. In Amazon EKS, AWS manages the underlying control-plane infrastructure, but platform engineers remain responsible for Kubernetes objects, access design, workload configuration, and operational diagnosis.\n\nThis section focuses on Control Plane Watches, Events, and Reconciliation. Describe watch streams, resource versions, event generation, controller work queues, retries, and how asynchronous reconciliation drives cluster behavior. The component or workflow must be understood as part of the complete Kubernetes request and reconciliation lifecycle rather than as an isolated feature. Engineers should identify which system owns each operation, which API object or AWS resource represents it, and which observable signal proves that it is functioning correctly.\n\n### Internal workflow and component interaction\nA typical operational flow begins when a user, controller, or automation tool submits a request through the Kubernetes API. The request is authenticated, authorized, validated, and processed by the relevant Kubernetes component. State changes are persisted where required, and controllers or node-level agents act on the resulting desired state. In EKS, AWS-managed infrastructure and customer-managed resources participate at different layers. Understanding these boundaries prevents engineers from troubleshooting an AWS-managed component as though it were a customer-operated Pod or EC2 process.\n\n### Practical implementation and verification\nUse the following command as a starting point:\nkubectl get events --all-namespaces --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n\nRun commands with the correct AWS profile, region, cluster context, namespace, and permissions. Inspect the returned 